{
  "_doc": "Runnable sample inputs for the matrix. These say what to try, never what the result is: every cell comes from a test. Attribute keys are the HTML names from the data packages; `prop` is the name a node stores (React's) and `html` is what must appear in the emitted markup. `elementAttributes` covers the attributes of single elements in HTML, SVG and MathML: `reactProps` names the prop where it is not the markup name in camelCase (React has no camelCase form for text-overflow and white-space as SVG attributes), `booleans` are rendered from true, and `values` holds a value where the default \"probe\" would not reach the page. `eventHandlersWithoutSpecLink` lists the handlers browser-compat-data knows as events without a handler- link; the browser probe compares the whole list with Chromium. `cssValues` is one value per entry of mdn-data types, functions and units, on a host property, and the browser probe proves each one valid. `selectors` are in the stored form, starting with `&`, and a bare pseudo-class row is tried as `&` plus its id. `selectorWrappers` names the `at` a selector row needs to mean anything. `webkitOnlySelectors` are rows Chromium refuses and WebKit parses, so they count, and the browser probe fails if Chromium starts to parse one. `definitionBodies` holds what a definition row stores beside its `atRules` prelude, and `marker` is the text its block must show. `pageLevelRows` stay failing under `page-level-css`: the route owns what they apply to. `mediaFeaturesChromiumCannotEvaluate` are standard media features this Chromium keeps in a sheet and evaluates to neither true nor false, and the browser probe fails if it starts to know one.",
